The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
static void atmio16d_detach(struct comedi_device *dev)
{
	reset_atmio16d(dev);
	comedi_legacy_detach(dev);
}
```
Reason:
The `reset_atmio16d(dev)` call uses `dev->iobase` (default 0) and `dev->private` (could be NULL) without checking whether the device was successfully initialized during `atmio16d_attach()`. If the attach handler fails partway through, these fields may be uninitialized, leading to writes to the wrong I/O ports and a null pointer dereference.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
static void atmio16d_detach(struct comedi_device *dev)
{
	if (dev->private)
		reset_atmio16d(dev);
	comedi_legacy_detach(dev);
}
```
Reason:
The added `if (dev->private)` condition ensures `reset_atmio16d(dev)` is only called when `dev->private` has been allocated (which implies `dev->iobase` was also set correctly, since that is done before allocating `dev->private`). This prevents both the invalid I/O port writes and the null pointer dereference when the attach handler failed.

